---

<DocsLayout
  title="Context Graph"
  description="Understand how Modesto connects code, sessions, checkpoints, and decisions so project context survives individual agent runs."
  currentPath="/docs/context-graph"
  eyebrow="The workspace"
  {toc}
>
  <section id="available-now">
    <h2>Available now: on the Pull Requests tab</h2>
    <p>
      The first real surface of the Context Graph ships today under Pull
      Requests. Open any pull request and its <strong>Context</strong> tab
      walks the relationships Modesto already records for that branch —
      threads that touched it, threads forked or side-chatted from those,
      and any cross-agent handoffs between them — and lists them with their
      notes and latest file changes.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-callout">
      <strong>Continue with this context, in one click.</strong>
      <p>
        The panel's "Continue with this context" action starts a new thread
        seeded with a summary of everything linked — prior notes, handoff
        summaries, unfinished steps, and changed files — so the next agent
        picks up where the last one left off instead of re-deriving it.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      This is branch- and thread-level linkage, built from data Modesto
      already tracks (no separate indexing step). The broader model below —
      file- and symbol-level relationships, dependency tracking — is the
      direction this is heading, not something every part of Modesto does
      yet.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="connections">
    <h2>What the Context Graph connects</h2>
    <p>
      A transcript tells you what was said. The Context Graph records how the
      work relates: which session changed a file, which checkpoint captured
      that state, which decision shaped the implementation, and what depends on
      the affected code.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <div><strong>Files and functions</strong><span>Code locations touched or referenced during a task.</span></div>
      <div><strong>Dependencies</strong><span>Imports, callers, packages, and project relationships that give a change its technical neighborhood.</span></div>
      <div><strong>Sessions and runs</strong><span>The provider activity that inspected, changed, tested, or discussed the code.</span></div>
      <div><strong>Checkpoints</strong><span>Durable seams that bind Git state to incomplete work and a recommended next action.</span></div>
      <div><strong>Decisions</strong><span>Constraints and conclusions that explain why the current implementation exists.</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="construction">
    <h2>How context is built</h2>
    <div class="docs-steps">
      <article><div><h3>Observe the task</h3><p>Modesto records provider events, tool activity, file references, Git changes, and review findings in the thread timeline.</p></div></article>
      <article><div><h3>Resolve project entities</h3><p>References are associated with repository paths and code symbols where the available evidence is strong enough.</p></div></article>
      <article><div><h3>Preserve durable seams</h3><p>Checkpoints connect the observed work to intent, validation state, unresolved questions, and the next action.</p></div></article>
      <article><div><h3>Carry relationships forward</h3><p>Later sessions can retrieve the relevant project history without replaying every prior transcript into the provider.</p></div></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="using">
    <h2>Using the graph during a task</h2>
    <p>
      Open context from a project, task, checkpoint, or code location. Follow
      relationships to answer practical questions: what changed this symbol,
      which run introduced the decision, what remains incomplete, and which
      checkpoint is safe to continue from.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-callout"><strong>Context is selected, not dumped.</strong><p>The graph helps Modesto retrieve the connected evidence for the current task. It is not an excuse to send an entire project history to every agent turn.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section id="boundaries">
    <h2>Boundaries and confidence</h2>
    <p>
      Graph relationships come from observable project and session evidence.
      Generated files, dynamic imports, uncommitted external changes, or work
      performed outside Modesto can leave gaps. Treat inferred relationships as
      navigation aids and verify the repository before making a high-impact
      change.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
